course: dictionary
lesson: 3201
title: Engage: taking part and catching attention
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «engage» has more than one meaning, so the words around it show you which one to use. «The story engaged the class.» The story caught the class's attention and kept them interested, so engage means to hold someone's interest.
explain: Try your meaning in the sentence. "The story kept the class interested" makes sense, so you have found the right meaning. Always check by swapping in your meaning.
explain: Engage can also mean to take part. «Engage in a discussion» means join in and think about the topic with others. Engage can also mean to fit together and start working, as in «The gears engage.»
fact: Engage means to take part in something, or to catch and keep someone's interest.
fact: «Engage in» means to take part: «engage in a discussion».
fact: «Engaged» can mean promised to marry: «Being engaged means two people have promised to marry each other.»
fact: In the UK, a phone or toilet that is «engaged» is busy or occupied.
fact: Gears that engage fit together and start working, so they can turn wheels.
exercise: «The story engaged the class.» What does engaged mean here? | *Caught and kept their interest | Shouted loudly | Left the room quickly | Bored and yawning
exercise: «Being engaged means two people have promised to marry each other.» What does engaged mean in this sentence? | Angry at each other | Parked in a garage | Busy with a task | *Promised to marry
exercise: «Judges give points for engaging fairly with the other side.» What does engaging mean here? | Running away from the other side | Ignoring the other side | Sleeping during the other side's turn | *Taking part fairly with the other side
test: «The gears engage.» What does engage mean here? | Break into small pieces | Make a loud noise | *Fit together and start working | Stop and rest
test: On a UK toilet door, the sign says «engaged». What does it mean? | *Busy and occupied | Ready to be cleaned | Promised to marry | Out of order
exam: Which phrase means to take part in a discussion? | engage the brake | engaged to be married | engage a builder | *engage in
